     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order yet because the dose looks unsafe as written.\n\n**Metformin 1700 mg PO three times daily is 5,100 mg/day.** The maximum labeled dose for immediate-release metformin is 2,550 mg/day, and most references cap it at 2,000 to 2,550 mg/day. Also, 1700 mg isn't a standard tablet strength (common strengths are 500, 850, and 1000 mg), so this may be a transcription error.\n\nSome possibilities:\n- **850 mg PO TID** (2,550 mg/day, the labeled maximum)\n- **1000 mg PO BID** (2,000 mg/day)\n- **500 mg PO TID** (1,500 mg/day, a common starting-to-moderate dose)\n- **1700 mg PO BID** (3,400 mg/day), if you intend a higher dose, though this is still above the usual maximum\n\nPlease confirm the dose and frequency, or tell me if you want the order entered as written. I'll enter it once you confirm. Also, the patient's 70 kg weight and normal renal function are fine for metformin, but I'd check the current eGFR before the first dose."
      }
     ],
